            <figcaption>{f.cap}</figcaption>
          </figure>
        ); })()}
        <h4 class="cag-h4">How Much Does an African Grey Parrot Cost, All In?</h4>
        <p>All in, expect {range(yr1Ours)} for your first year with a documented Grey — the bird itself, delivery, the cage and stand you buy once, the first avian-vet visit, and a year of food and enrichment. That is the number worth planning against, and it is roughly double the sticker price on any route you choose.</p>

        <h5 class="cag-h5">Why a Single Price Never Survives Contact With a Real Aviary</h5>
        <p>Every clutch differs. A Timneh hen weaning early in a quiet season and a Congo cock hand-fed through a busy one do not cost us the same to raise, and pretending otherwise would mean overcharging for one to subsidise the other. We price each bird for what that bird actually took.</p>

        <h6 class="cag-h6">From the Ledger: Our Floor Price Has Not Moved Since 2014</h6>
        <p>We have never placed an African Grey below {money(floorPrice)} in twelve years of breeding, through a market that roughly tripled after 2020. The floor is not a promotion and it is not a negotiating position — it is the point below which the documentation, the screening and the hand-rearing stop paying for themselves.</p>

        <h3 class="cag-h3">Where Do Our Congo and Timneh Prices Sit in That Range?</h3>
        <p>Our Congos run {money(1700)} to {money(2500)} and our Timnehs {money(1500)} to {money(1600)}, which places every bird we hold in the lower-middle of the national spread. Our full <a href="/african-grey-parrot-price/">cost-of-ownership breakdown</a> carries the long-term arithmetic; this page stays on the money that changes hands to acquire the bird.</p>

        <h4 class="cag-h4">Does a Timneh Grey Start Lower Than a Congo?</h4>
        <p>Yes, by about two hundred dollars at the entry point — and that gap says nothing about the bird. <a href="/timneh-african-grey-for-sale/">Timneh pricing starts lower</a> because Timnehs are smaller, wean a little sooner and are less in demand than the red-tailed Congo, not because they talk less or bond less. Several of our steadiest birds have been Timnehs.</p>
