      <!-- 02 · RESCUE FEES -->
      <section id="rescue" class="sec">
        <h2 class="cag-h2">What Do Rescues Actually Charge to Adopt an African Grey?</h2>
        <p>Parrot rescues publish their fees, which makes this the one route you can price without asking anybody: the schedules we checked list African Greys between $450 and $1,500. We are a breeder, not a rescue, and we would rather send you to a good one with accurate numbers than pretend the route does not exist — our <a href="/african-grey-adoption/">honest guide to where adoption actually works</a> covers the application side in full.</p>

        {(() => { const f = fig("inf7"); return (
          <figure class="sec-fig">
            <img class={`sec-img ${f.kind === "inf" ? "inf-img" : "og-photo"}`} src={f.src} srcset={f.srcset}
              sizes="(max-width:900px) 92vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768"
              style={f.kind === "og" ? `object-position:${f.pos}` : undefined}
              loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt={f.alt} />
            <figcaption>{f.cap}</figcaption>
          </figure>
        ); })()}
        <h3 class="cag-h3">What Do the Published Adoption Fee Schedules Say?</h3>
        <p>Four organisations publish a figure for this species, and they do not agree with one another. That disagreement is the useful part: it tells you the fee reflects the organisation's costs and region, not the bird in front of you.</p>

        <h4 class="cag-h4">How Much Is an African Grey Adoption Fee?</h4>
        <p>Between $450 and $1,500, depending entirely on who you ask. <a href="https://www.parrotsfirst.org/adoption-donation-fees" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Parrots First fee schedule</a> lists African Greys at $450, <a href="https://www.phoenixlanding.org/adoption-fees.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Phoenix Landing publishes $500</a> for greys and large macaws, and <a href="https://www.riparrots.org/current-fees" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rhode Island Parrot Rescue lists $1,500</a> — which lands exactly on our own floor price.</p>

        <h5 class="cag-h5">Every Fee Schedule We Checked Lists a Different Number</h5>
        <p>A $450 grey and a $1,500 grey from two rescues are not different grades of bird. They are different organisations recovering different overheads, in different states, with different intake volumes. Read the fee as a contribution to a programme rather than a valuation of an animal.</p>

        <h6 class="cag-h6">Receipt Note: Parrots First $450, Phoenix Landing $500, Rhode Island $1,500</h6>
        <p>Phoenix Landing states plainly on its own fee page that the adoption fee is minimal compared with the cost of caring for a parrot, and lists what it does not cover — food, veterinary care, enrichment, and a spacious cage. That is a rescue making the same argument this page makes, on its own website, without a breeder's incentive to make it.</p>

        <h3 class="cag-h3">Why Is the Fee You Pay Often Higher Than the Fee Published?</h3>
        <p>Published schedules describe organised rescues. Most greys that change hands never go near one — they move privately, between owners, at whatever figure the current owner names, and that figure is routinely higher than any schedule.</p>

        <h4 class="cag-h4">What Is a Rehoming Fee, and Is It Different From an Adoption Fee?</h4>
        <p>It is different in every way that matters. <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/parrots/comments/133zdo2/rescue_african_grey_price/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">One widely-read r/parrots thread</a> opens with a San Diego bird store asking a $5,500 "rehoming fee" for a six-year-old tame hen with a thin history, while selling hand-tame babies at $6,000 — and the sharpest reply in the thread points out that rehoming is simply a euphemism for selling.</p>

        <h5 class="cag-h5">A Tame Adult in a High-Demand Metro Can Cost More Than a Baby</h5>
        <p>Owners in that same discussion report paying $600 to $900 for adult greys, and $900 with a cage included in a second thread. The number tracks the local market and the bird's tameness, not the word on the listing. If a fee is high, ask what history comes with it; if it is low, ask the same question harder.</p>

        <h6 class="cag-h6">From the Ledger: CITES Appendix I Slows Every Rescue Transfer Down</h6>
        <p>Parrots First routes any CITES Appendix I species to case-by-case committee review — and its schedule predates the 2017 uplisting that moved African Greys into that appendix at CoP17. The paperwork we handle as routine is the same paperwork that makes a careful rescue slow down, which is one honest reason adoption timelines run long.</p>

        <p class="sec-cta"><a class="btn-clay" href="#reserve">See what {money(floorPrice)} buys here &rarr;</a></p>
