      <!-- 06 · UNDER $1,000 -->
      <section id="cheap" class="sec">
        <h2 class="cag-h2">Why Do Some African Greys Cost Under $1,000?</h2>
        <p>Because something has been left out, and it is worth knowing what. A Grey advertised at $600 or $800 is either a genuine private rehoming with thin paperwork, or it is not a bird at all — and the two look identical in a listing photograph.</p>

        {(() => { const f = fig("inf5"); return (
          <figure class="sec-fig">
            <img class={`sec-img ${f.kind === "inf" ? "inf-img" : "og-photo"}`} src={f.src} srcset={f.srcset}
              sizes="(max-width:900px) 92vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768"
              style={f.kind === "og" ? `object-position:${f.pos}` : undefined}
              loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt={f.alt} />
            <figcaption>{f.cap}</figcaption>
          </figure>
        ); })()}
        <h3 class="cag-h3">What Are You Actually Buying at a Sub-Floor Price?</h3>
        <p>Usually you are buying the absence of three things: a DNA certificate, a disease-screening panel, and a documented origin. None of those absences show up in the photograph, and all three surface within the first month.</p>

        <h4 class="cag-h4">Is a Cheap African Grey Parrot a Scam?</h4>
        <p>Sometimes yes, and the tells are consistent. <a href="/how-to-avoid-african-grey-parrot-scams/">Listings priced below our floor</a> repeat the same patterns we documented across the price SERP: manufactured strike-through discounts on a living animal, the word "adopt" used over priced stock to soften a sale, flat pricing across wildly different species, and a seller who moves to payment before they will move to a video call.</p>

        <h5 class="cag-h5">An Undocumented Bird Costs More After It Arrives Than It Saved</h5>
        <p>Run the arithmetic honestly. Saving $900 on the purchase, then paying for sexing, a full disease panel, a quarantine period and a first-visit workup, lands you above what a documented bird cost — with a bird whose history still cannot be reconstructed.</p>

        <h6 class="cag-h6">Receipt Note: We Have Never Sold a Grey Below Our {money(floorPrice)} Floor</h6>
        <p>Not in twelve years, and not when a market that tripled would have let us. If you find a bird advertised as ours below that figure, it is not ours — check the <a href="/contact-us/">contact details on our own site</a> against whatever the listing shows you, because that mismatch is the single fastest scam check there is.</p>

        <p class="sec-cta"><a class="btn-clay" href="#reserve">Compare that against a papered bird &rarr;</a></p>
      </section>

      <div class="seam"><img src={seam} width="150" height="50" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div>

      <!-- 07 · GETTING HOME + the US-only counter-position -->
      <section id="shipping" class="sec">
        <h2 class="cag-h2">Where Do We Ship, and What Does Delivery Add?</h2>
        <p>We place birds inside the United States only, at {money(airport)} for airport pickup or {money(home)} for home delivery, with a flight-nanny option from {money(nanny)}. Delivery is the last line on the day-one figure and the one people most often forget to budget.</p>

        {(() => { const f = fig("inf6"); return (
          <figure class="sec-fig">
            <img class={`sec-img ${f.kind === "inf" ? "inf-img" : "og-photo"}`} src={f.src} srcset={f.srcset}
              sizes="(max-width:900px) 92vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768"
              style={f.kind === "og" ? `object-position:${f.pos}` : undefined}
              loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt={f.alt} />
            <figcaption>{f.cap}</figcaption>
          </figure>
        ); })()}
        <h3 class="cag-h3">Do You Ship African Greys Outside the United States?</h3>
        <p>No, and we would rather say so on this page than in a reply three emails deep. We are a USDA-licensed Texas aviary; every bird we place stays within the country.</p>

        <h4 class="cag-h4">What Does an African Grey Parrot Cost in the USA Versus Abroad?</h4>
        <p>The figures are not comparable, and it matters because overseas prices circulate widely in search results. A US captive-bred Grey carries CITES Appendix I documentation, USDA-licensed origin, PCR screening and an avian-vet certificate; a price quoted in another market reflects a different legal regime, a different currency and often a different chain of custody entirely.</p>

        <h5 class="cag-h5">Foreign Price Figures Do Not Transfer to a US Captive-Bred Grey</h5>
        <p>Because African Greys sit in CITES Appendix I, moving one across a border lawfully needs an export permit from the origin country and an import permit from the destination — paperwork that takes months and routinely costs more than the bird. When a US buyer compares our price against an overseas figure, they are comparing two different products that share a species name.</p>

        <h3 class="cag-h3">What Does It Cost to Get Your Grey Home?</h3>
        <p>Three tiers, and one of them is free. Our <a href="/buy-african-grey-parrots-with-shipping/">full nationwide shipping guide</a> walks the whole journey; the short version is below.</p>

        <h4 class="cag-h4">How Much Is Shipping for an African Grey Parrot?</h4>
        <div class="ship-cards">
          <div class="ship-c"><p class="ship-tier">Airport pickup</p><p class="ship-price">{money(airport)}</p><p class="ship-body">IATA live-animal cargo with Delta, United or American to your nearest major airport.</p></div>
          <div class="ship-c"><p class="ship-tier">Home delivery</p><p class="ship-price">{money(home)}</p><p class="ship-body">Door to door, with the documentation folder handed over on arrival.</p></div>
          <div class="ship-c"><p class="ship-tier">Flight nanny</p><p class="ship-price">from {money(nanny)}</p><p class="ship-body">In-cabin with a vetted chaperone; quoted per route because it covers their round-trip airfare.</p></div>
        </div>
        <p class="ship-line">Ships nationwide · {money(airport)} airport · {money(home)} home</p>

        <h5 class="cag-h5">Pickup in Midland Costs Nothing If You Are Within Three Hours</h5>
        <p>Plenty of Texas families drive. If you are inside two to three hours of Midland you can collect in person, meet the parent birds, and hand over nothing for transport — which also happens to be the best possible way to verify a breeder.</p>

        <h6 class="cag-h6">From the Ledger: Airport {money(airport)}, Home {money(home)}, Flight Nanny From {money(nanny)}</h6>
        <div class="geo-cards">
          {shipPlaces.map((p) => (
            <a class="geo-c" href={p.href}>
              <span class="geo-kw">{p.label}</span>
              <span class="geo-note">{p.note}</span>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="seam"><img src={seam} width="150" height="50" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div>

      <!-- 08 · RESERVABLE NOW — Avail-B faceted by PRICE BAND -->
      <section id="reserve" class="sec">
        <h2 class="cag-h2">Which African Greys Can You Reserve Right Now?</h2>
        <p>We hold {available.length} documented birds today, priced {range([floorPrice, ceilPrice])}, and every one carries the same paperwork regardless of where it sits in that range. Filter by what you can spend rather than by what you like the look of — the cheapest bird here is not the least documented one.</p>

        <h3 class="cag-h3">Which Bird Fits Which Budget?</h3>
        <div class="availB">
          <aside class="availB-side" aria-label="Filter available African Greys by price band">
